a private funeral for civil rights  leader and long-time congressman  john lewis will be held next thursday in  atlanta  it will follow a week-long celebration  of his life  cbs this morning saturday co-host  michelle miller is at the edmond pettis  bridge in  selma alabama where lewis was beaten by  state troopers back in 1965. michelle  good morning  good morning as you said it will be a  full week of remembrances  and special moments beginning on  saturday  in the congressman's hometown of troy  alabama that's about  90 miles southeast of where i am there  will be a memorial at troy university  where lewis was denied entry  because of his race and where the local  library  wouldn't even issue him a library card  indignities that fueled his fighting  spirit  lewis's body will then head here to  selma of course the edmond pettis bridge  is the scene of the infamous bloody  sunday  some 55 years ago where he was badly  beaten during the march to montgomery  on sunday a processional will carry  lewis across the historic bridge before  he's taken to the alabama state capitol  that will certainly be an emotional  highlight  and on monday and tuesday lewis's body  will lie in state at the u.s  capitol building first in the rotunda  and then with social distancing rules in  effect on the east front steps for  public viewing  it's a full circle tour of the places  and the people to whom  he meant so much and we can tell you the  crew and i  and driving from georgia through the  alabama countryside  that reverence was on full display in  the church message boards  and the flags flying half staff at  public squares and barbecue joints alike  this congressman was a hero to so many  people  both big and small gail  and he deserves michelle quite the  send-off  it sounds like they have a lot planned  very very very well deserved  thank you very much michelle miller  reporting from selma
 

